# Testing Conventions

Living rules for unit/component tests. Prefer matching the user’s style; if a preference conflicts with a hard constraint (security, correctness, Next.js/App Router), prioritize the constraint and note the trade-off.

## Create / update / change / delete

Rules for creating, updating, changing, or deleting unit/component tests: [`.cursor/commands/update-unit-tests.mdc`](../commands/update-unit-tests.mdc).

## Layout and naming

- Place tests in a `tests/` folder **next to** the file under test (same directory level as that service/component/module). All tests for that level live inside `tests/`.
- Test file name: `<name-of-file-under-test>.tests.ts` or `.tests.tsx`.
- **One** test file per module under test (do not split one service/component across multiple test files).
- Example: `src/front-end/services/finance.service.ts` → `src/front-end/services/tests/finance.service.tests.ts`
- Example: `src/front-end/components/tile-header/tile-header.tsx` → `src/front-end/components/tile-header/tests/tile-header.tests.tsx`

## Structure

- Top-level `describe` = name of the service / class / component under test.
- Nested `describe` blocks = each public method (or distinct behavior chunk if there are no methods).
- Group tests with `describe` by method or behavior.
- Prefer **one assertion / one condition per test** (one logical outcome per `it`).
- Use **Arrange → Act → Assert**, and mark each phase with comments (`// Arrange`, `// Act`, `// Assert`). When setup and call are one expression, use `// Arrange & Act`.
- Never call `private` / `protected` members directly — test only through the public API.

```ts
it("returns 0 when current is NaN", () => {
  // Arrange & Act
  const result = financeService.calculateProgressPercentage(
    Number.NaN,
    100,
  );

  // Assert
  expect(result).toBe(0);
});

it("formats positive amounts with a plus sign", () => {
  // Arrange
  const amount = 12.5;

  // Act
  const result = transactionService.getTransactionAmount(amount);

  // Assert
  expect(result).toBe("+$12.50");
});
```


## F.I.R.S.T.

- **Fast** — milliseconds; no slow I/O that blocks regular local runs.
- **Independent** — each test runs alone; order must not matter; no shared mutable state between tests.
- **Repeatable** — same result every run; no dependence on clock, network, or machine environment.
- **Self-validating** — clear pass/fail via assertions; no manual log or visual checks.
- **Thorough** — happy path plus edges, invalid input, errors/exceptions (not only the ideal case).

(Classic literature sometimes uses **Timely** for T; in this project prefer **Thorough** coverage as above.)

## Mocks and data

- Mock external services; **never** make real API / network / DB calls in unit/component tests.
- Shared mocks/stubs used by multiple test files live under the matching layer:
  - `src/front-end/lib/test-utils/`
  - `src/back-end/lib/test-utils/`
  - `src/shared/lib/test-utils/` (for shared-module tests)
- Shared util names are free-form (not required to match `*.tests.ts`), e.g. `logger.service.mock.ts`, `fetch.mock.ts`, `db.stub.ts`, `component-name.mock.ts`.
- Example: `src/shared/lib/test-utils/logger.service.mock.ts` for:

```ts
vi.mock("@/shared/services/logger.service", () => ({
  default: {
    logError: vi.fn(),
    logInfo: vi.fn(),
    logWarn: vi.fn(),
  },
}));
```

- **Never** use real credentials, admin accounts, secrets, `.env` values, or production-like sensitive data. Use synthetic fixture data only.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD — fake credentials
validationService.validateSignInSchema({
  email: "user@example.com",
  password: "secret",
});

// ❌ BAD — real login / admin / secret material
```

## Stack and commands

- Runner: Vitest; components: React Testing Library. See [readme-dev.md](../../readme-dev.md) Testing section.
- Agents / CI / one-shot: `npm run test:run`
- Local watch (humans): `npm run test`
- Do **not** use `npm run test` in automated agent or CI flows — Vitest watch does not exit.
